{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GFf9vX8gTr4GRuZ719grNL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 04:10 +07\n2\t# Task: Architect rulings for slice 6c (read the code, rule, do NOT implement)\n3\tYou are claude-fable-5-1, NEW session. Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c`, branch `srvm-phase6c` (HEAD 47ddb1d9: opus's 6c, committed). You may edit ONLY your report `collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md` and, if you need to quote amended design wording, put it IN THE REPORT (do not edit docs). Vocabulary per UCF 7.5.3. Read: codex's review `collab/1792350000000-review-6c-layouts.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md` (F1-F6, probes, and its D1-D5 amendment wording), opus's report `collab/1792340000000-engineer-6c-layouts.report.md`, the amended design `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` (sections 4, 5.5, 5.6, 8.3 and the open questions in 11), `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, and the harness lanes in `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`. Owner rulings that bind you: A (semantic address) is the ONLY code identity, R and H are derived checksums, never names; the AST is never lost and always retrievable; sharing of cursor cells survives transport; BLAKE3; no backward compatibility; Phase 6 does NOT edit kernels (a seam in a kernel needs an explicit decision and its own small slice, like 6c-pre); old stack and de Bruijn compilers and kernels are deleted at phase 8 (so effort spent on them has a short shelf life: say this where it matters).\n4\tRule on each, with the cheapest option that keeps the invariants, and cost in days:\n5\tR1 (F2) `:ambiguous-image`: `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)` have different A and the same H and R, so a native-kernel state holding both cannot name its A. Options: (a) refuse as now but at lift preflight before attach and say it is a documented limit until the cutover (the stack and de Bruijn kernels die at phase 8 anyway); (b) retain A provenance through a kernel seam; (c) other. Does the semantic-register kernel (the survivor) have this problem at all? Rule, and state what to test.\n6\tR2 (F3) the 89/534 matrix: rows 7-12 and 17 (link, install) and row 4 (retained FFI) are excluded on the native lanes (law 4 61 of 89 cases, law 5 350 of 534 pairs, tail acceptance rows 6 and 20 only). Because the native kernels link by H and R while module responses carry A, and install children have no native layout, and the retained FFI re-park names the kernel's own pair (D2). Options: (a) amend section 8.3 and 5.2 to make the exclusion normative with the reason (the native lanes are a cross-engine check of the shared fragment, and die at phase 8), keep the semantic-register reference lane covering all rows; (b) land a seam slice for the two de Bruijn kernels (retained FFI response pair, per codex's D2 wording) now; (c) both. State the exact new counts if (a). Which is the cheapest honest closure, given the native kernels are deleted at phase 8?\n7\tR3 (F1) free-env values on the native lanes: convert them in both directions; any decision needed beyond what codex wrote? State the test.\n8\tR4 (F4) law 4 negative controls and `canonical-under` erasing the descriptor: rule what `canonical-under` may erase (D1), and whether `logical-positions` is acceptable with the controls codex lists.\n9\tR5 (F5, D3) the unique-preimage rule for `virt` outside live-out: adopt codex's wording or amend; ruling on the allowed refusal kind for an ambiguous upward tamper (section 8.3 item 12).\n10\tR6 D1-D5 amendment wording: accept codex's text or give yours (verbatim, by section) so the next doc pass is mechanical. Also: the two new `assoc` sites on `:store` in layout.cljc (`canonical-task`, `kernel-task`) tripped the store-write audit: say whether they should go through `engine/store-put` (reserved-key check) or the audit allowlist with a reason, and why.\n11\tForeground only. Report: 12-line plain summary first (what to do, what it costs, what we give up), then each ruling with exact wording, then a work list for opus ordered by priority with acceptance tests, then open questions that need the OWNER (scope reductions that change the acceptance counts must be flagged as owner decisions). End READY.\n12\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0da6a8ed-da80-4f95-b938-953b362e5554","uuid":"6d811b69-935a-4095-aa69-c1830bad7e3f","timestamp":"2026-10-12T18:26:10.092Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c/collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.prompt.md","content":"Created-Local: 2026-10-13 04:10 +07\n# Task: Architect rulings for slice 6c (read the code, rule, do NOT implement)\nYou are claude-fable-5-1, NEW session. Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c`, branch `srvm-phase6c` (HEAD 47ddb1d9: opus's 6c, committed). You may edit ONLY your report `collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md` and, if you need to quote amended design wording, put it IN THE REPORT (do not edit docs). Vocabulary per UCF 7.5.3. Read: codex's review `collab/1792350000000-review-6c-layouts.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md` (F1-F6, probes, and its D1-D5 amendment wording), opus's report `collab/1792340000000-engineer-6c-layouts.report.md`, the amended design `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` (sections 4, 5.5, 5.6, 8.3 and the open questions in 11), `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, and the harness lanes in `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`. Owner rulings that bind you: A (semantic address) is the ONLY code identity, R and H are derived checksums, never names; the AST is never lost and always retrievable; sharing of cursor cells survives transport; BLAKE3; no backward compatibility; Phase 6 does NOT edit kernels (a seam in a kernel needs an explicit decision and its own small slice, like 6c-pre); old stack and de Bruijn compilers and kernels are deleted at phase 8 (so effort spent on them has a short shelf life: say this where it matters).\nRule on each, with the cheapest option that keeps the invariants, and cost in days:\nR1 (F2) `:ambiguous-image`: `(fn [x] x)` and `(fn [y] y)` have different A and the same H and R, so a native-kernel state holding both cannot name its A. Options: (a) refuse as now but at lift preflight before attach and say it is a documented limit until the cutover (the stack and de Bruijn kernels die at phase 8 anyway); (b) retain A provenance through a kernel seam; (c) other. Does the semantic-register kernel (the survivor) have this problem at all? Rule, and state what to test.\nR2 (F3) the 89/534 matrix: rows 7-12 and 17 (link, install) and row 4 (retained FFI) are excluded on the native lanes (law 4 61 of 89 cases, law 5 350 of 534 pairs, tail acceptance rows 6 and 20 only). Because the native kernels link by H and R while module responses carry A, and install children have no native layout, and the retained FFI re-park names the kernel's own pair (D2). Options: (a) amend section 8.3 and 5.2 to make the exclusion normative with the reason (the native lanes are a cross-engine check of the shared fragment, and die at phase 8), keep the semantic-register reference lane covering all rows; (b) land a seam slice for the two de Bruijn kernels (retained FFI response pair, per codex's D2 wording) now; (c) both. State the exact new counts if (a). Which is the cheapest honest closure, given the native kernels are deleted at phase 8?\nR3 (F1) free-env values on the native lanes: convert them in both directions; any decision needed beyond what codex wrote? State the test.\nR4 (F4) law 4 negative controls and `canonical-under` erasing the descriptor: rule what `canonical-under` may erase (D1), and whether `logical-positions` is acceptable with the controls codex lists.\nR5 (F5, D3) the unique-preimage rule for `virt` outside live-out: adopt codex's wording or amend; ruling on the allowed refusal kind for an ambiguous upward tamper (section 8.3 item 12).\nR6 D1-D5 amendment wording: accept codex's text or give yours (verbatim, by section) so the next doc pass is mechanical. Also: the two new `assoc` sites on `:store` in layout.cljc (`canonical-task`, `kernel-task`) tripped the store-write audit: say whether they should go through `engine/store-put` (reserved-key check) or the audit allowlist with a reason, and why.\nForeground only. Report: 12-line plain summary first (what to do, what it costs, what we give up), then each ruling with exact wording, then a work list for opus ordered by priority with acceptance tests, then open questions that need the OWNER (scope reductions that change the acceptance counts must be flagged as owner decisions). End READY.\n","numLines":12,"startLine":1,"totalLines":12}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GFf9vX8gTr4GRuZ719grNL","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
