{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jv3mG2ivzpNNwPp1vdNoTS","type":"tool_result","content":"469\t## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame\n470\t\n471\t### 4.1 What a layout is\n472\t\n473\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.3; UCF §7.4.2). A foreign engine publishes a\n474\tstatic map per segment and engine (`A`, resume pcs, `def`/`use`/`L`\n475\tfrom `analysis/analyze`, `lexically-required`, and a layout from\n476\tphysical location to virtual register) and reconstructs the canonical\n477\tframe from its state at a safepoint; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` on\n478\tadmitted canonical frames. Two kernels are intended to reconstruct\n479\tcanonical frames: the de Bruijn register kernel over `R(A)` and the de\n480\tBruijn stack kernel over `H(A)` (phase 5). The AST walker is not (frozen\n481\t§10 phase 6 names the three kernels; the walker has no pc).\n482\t\n483\t**PROPOSED** namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout`, file\n484\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, requiring\n485\t`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `.derive` (phase 5),\n486\t`yin.vm.debruijn` (`resolve-name`), `yin.vm.engine` (`bind-params`), and\n487\tthe two kernels' public state. Public: `register-layout`,\n488\t`stack-layout` (static, per segment), `lift-act`, `lift-tail`,\n489\t`lower-act`, `lower-tail` (per kernel, dispatching on the kernel's\n490\t`link-format`).\n491\t\n492\t### 4.2 The register kernel's layout\n493\t\n494\t**DECIDED** by phase-5 §2.4 (R's bodies and pcs are A's) and §2.3\n495\t(`R(v) = locals + slot(v)`); the kernel's payload shape from\n496\t`debruijn/register.cljc:690-704` (call frame: `:site-pc :return-pc\n497\t:frames :regs :live :dest :store-of`) and `write-back` (`:455-485`:\n498\t`:write-result` rebuilds a dense register vector from `regs` and writes\n499\t`dest`; `:return-result` runs the return transition).\n500\t\n501\t**Static half.** For segment `A` with `R = (:image (derive/register-image\n502\tv))`: for every pc `p`, `v[p]` names virtual registers in its `rd`,\n503\t`:reg` and `:regs` slots and `R[p]` names physical registers in the same\n504\tslots. `phys(body, v)` is read off any pc that names `v`; it is total on\n505\tthe virtuals a body names. Its inverse is **restricted**: `virt(p, s)`\n506\tis defined only for `s` in the physical image of\n507\t`live-out*(p) = ⋃ L(q) for q ∈ (analysis/successors v p)` (the actual\n508\tsuccessors, phase-5 §2.5; empty for a tail call) at a **reachable**\n509\tboundary pc `p` (`analysis/reachable`); there it is \"the `v ∈ live-out*(p)`\n510\twith `phys(v) = s`\", single-valued because the allocator's intervals\n511\tcover every live point and distinct live virtuals hold distinct slots\n512\t(a property the harness checks, §5.6 item 3, never assumed). Physical\n513\toperands of **unreachable** layout-syntax instructions (the structural\n514\tslot shared by several definition-less ids, phase-5 §2.2) are not\n515\tinverted; they are validated separately by the kernel's own\n516\t`register-image-defect` and by phase-5's gates, and never reach a\n517\tcanonical frame (no reachable instruction reads them).\n518\t\n519\t**Lift, `act`.** From the kernel's wait entry with `:resume-mode\n520\t:write-result` (payload `:image :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live\n521\t:continuation :dest :store-of`, v2 amendment §5.3):\n522\t\n523\t1. `A` = the A the image row of the entry's `:image` was derived from\n524\t   (the kernel's code space is keyed by A after phase 5; R is its\n525\t   checksum).\n526\t2. relative `pc` = the entry's `pc` minus the image row's offset\n527\t   (`image-row`/`absolute-pc`, `debruijn/register.cljc:326-337`); R's pcs\n528\t   are A's, so this is the canonical pc.\n529\t3. `rd = (code/rd (nth v (dec pc)))` **from A**, never the physical\n530\t   `:dest` (which is `phys(rd)`).\n531\t4. `W = {virt(pc−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ regs}`; the kernel's `live` is\n532\t   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(pc−1) rd)))` (the subtraction is on\n533\t   **virtual** ids before mapping: on the worked example `live-out*(3) =\n534\t   L(4) = {1 2}`, `(disj … 2) = {1}`, `phys → [0]`; subtracting the\n535\t   physical `3` from `{1 2}` would wrongly keep `2`), so `(keys W) =\n536\t   saved(pc, rd)`, which rule A5/A6 then checks.\n537\t5. `E` = §4.4 from the entry's `frames` and `store-of`, at `body(pc)`.\n538\t6. `K`: for each continuation frame `f`, **independently**: its\n539\t   `return-pc` is absolute in the kernel's concatenated code space, so\n540\t   first its image row `rowₖ = (image-row images return-pc)` and relative\n541\t   `pcₖ = return-pc − offsetₖ`, then `Aₖ` of that row (frames may cross\n542\t   images: a module closure called from another image returns into its\n543\t   caller's image), `rdₖ = (code/rd (nth vₖ (dec pcₖ)))` from `Aₖ`, never\n544\t   `f.dest`; `Wₖ = {virt_{Aₖ}(pcₖ−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ f.regs}`; `Eₖ` = §4.4\n545\t   from `f.frames` and `f.store-of` at `body(pcₖ)` in `Aₖ`.\n546\t7. pending: the engine fields of the entry, as `lift-pending!` maps them\n547\t   (`handoff.cljc:674-749`).\n548\t\n549\t**Lift, `tail`.** From an entry with `:resume-mode :return-result`\n550\t(`dest nil`, `live []`): `A` and the relative `site = site-pc` as in 1-2\n551\t(the entry's `site-pc` is the tail call; its `pc` is `site+1`); `K` as\n552\tin 6; pending as in 7; **no** `W`, `E` or `pc` are read: the entry's\n553\t`regs` (empty by `live []`) and `frames` are discarded (frozen §2.4: a\n554\ttail state carries no window and no `E`).\n555\t\n556\t**Lower, `act`.** Given a canonical `act` and the receiver's attached\n557\timage for `A` (derived by `derive/register-image` from the carried\n558\tvector, offset `off`): `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + pc − 1`;\n559\t`:pc = off + pc`; `:regs = (sort-by first [[phys(v) V] for [v V] in W])`;\n560\t`:live = (sort (map phys (keys W)))` (the kernel's `live-exact` rule\n561\trecomputes `body-liveness` and must agree); `:dest = phys(rd)`;\n562\t`:resume-mode :write-result`; `:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of`\n563\tfrom `E`; `:continuation` = for each `K[i]`, with its own `Aₖ`'s image\n564\trow `offₖ`: `{:site-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ −1) :return-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ) :regs\n565\t… :live … :dest (phys_{Aₖ} rdₖ) :frames (§4.4 from Eₖ) :store-of …}`;\n566\t`:format reffects/format-tag`, `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's current\n567\taggregate (never carried). Engine fields from the pending\n568\t(`lower-frame!`, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`), `:deliver` carried.\n569\t\n570\t**Lower, `tail`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + site`;\n571\t`:pc = off + site + 1`; `:regs []`; `:live []`; `:dest nil`;\n572\t`:resume-mode :return-result`; `:frames []` and `:store-of nil`\n573\t(unused: `write-back`'s `:return-result` branch reads `:frames` and\n574\t`:store-of` only to assign them before the return transition replaces\n575\tthem from the popped frame, `register.cljc:461-468`; the harness checks\n576\tthat the values chosen here are never observable, §5.6 item 8);\n577\t`:continuation` as for `act`. The register kernel already supports this\n578\tdelivery (`:return-result`, `register.cljc:458-468`).\n579\t\n580\t### 4.3 The stack kernel's layout\n581\t\n582\t**DECIDED** by phase-5 §3.2 (the emitter's walk) and the kernel's call\n583\tand return arms (`debruijn/stack.cljc:716-776`): a non-tail `:call`\n584\tpops `f` and the `argc` operands, leaving `stack'`, and pushes\n585\t`{:return-pc (inc pc) :frames frames :stack-base (count stack')}`\n586\t(`:728-733`); `:return` truncates to the frame's `stack-base` and pushes\n587\tthe value (`:766-772`); a host-function effect parks with the\n588\tcontinuation after the call site and `stack-restore` resumes by conjing\n589\tthe value at the entry's `pc` (`:748-752`, `:555-568`).\n590\t\n591\t**Static half.**\n592\t\n593\t1. **pc correspondence.** The stack emitter visits parse nodes in\n594\t   `project`'s order and emits one instruction per A instruction plus a\n595\t   `:push` after each child of a call, each `:stream-put` target and\n596\t   each `:ffi-call` operand (phase-5 §3.2). So H **with every `:push`\n597\t   removed** corresponds one-to-one, in order, with A: the k-th non-push\n598\t   H instruction ↔ A pc `k`; `h->a`, `a->h` are the two directions. The\n599\t   harness checks mnemonic families agree at every k (§5.6 item 4).\n600\t2. **operand layout.** For an A pc `p` and the parse node at `p`,\n601\t   `stack-layout(p)` is the vector, bottom to top, of the virtual\n602\t   registers whose values are pushed and not yet consumed while control\n603\t   is inside `p`'s expression: for each ancestor `n` and the child `c` on\n604\t   the path to `p`, the children of `n` before `c` that `n` pushes (a\n605\t   `:call`: the operator and the operands before `c`; a `:stream-put`:\n606\t   the target when `c` is the value; an `:ffi-call`: the operands before\n607\t   `c`; `:if`, `:define`, `:stream-cursor/next/close`, `:resume`: none).\n608\t   **Checked** (§5.6 item 5): for a **non-tail** parking instruction,\n609\t   `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p) (code/rd v[p]))`.\n610\t   For a **tail** `:call` the canonical state has no window and\n611\t   `stack-layout(p)` is not consulted (it may be non-empty for a tail\n612\t   call in operand position, whose pushed siblings are dead layout\n613\t   syntax).\n614\t\n615\t**K slices (corrected).** Let the kernel's continuation be\n616\t`[f₀ … f_{m−1}]` outermost first with bases `b₀ … b_{m−1}` and the\n617\toperand stack `St` of length `n`. Because `bᵢ = (count stack')` is the\n618\tcaller's residual height **after** popping the callee's operands, the\n619\tslice of operands belonging to activation `i` is\n620\t\n621\t```\n622\tslice(f₀)      = St[0, b₀)\n623\tslice(fᵢ)      = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)        for 0 < i < m\n624\tslice(current) = St[b_{m−1}, n)         ; or St[0, n) when K is empty\n625\t```\n626\t\n627\t(the review's correction: `[previous-base, base_i)` with previous-base\n628\t0, and the current activation `[last-base, count)`).\n629\t\n630\t**Lift, `act`.** From a wait entry `{:image :pc :frames :stack\n631\t:continuation :store-of}` (v2 amendment §5.2) whose `pc−1` in H is **not**\n632\ta tail `:call`:\n633\t\n634\t1. `A` from the image row; relative H pc `pcₕ = pc − off`; canonical\n635\t   `pc = h->a(pcₕ − 1) + 1`; `rd = (code/rd v[pc−1])`.\n636\t2. `W = (zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice(current))`; the two have\n637\t   equal length (checked) and `(keys W) = saved(pc, rd)` (rule A5/A6).\n638\t3. `E` = §4.4 from `frames` and `store-of` at `body(pc)`.\n639\t4. `K`: for `fᵢ` **independently**: its `return-pc` is absolute; row and\n640\t   relative `pcₕᵢ` through the stack kernel's own offset table\n641\t   (`debruijn/stack.cljc:355`, `absolute-pc`'s inverse); `Aᵢ` of that row;\n642\t   `pcᵢ = h->a_{Aᵢ}(pcₕᵢ − 1) + 1`; `rdᵢ = (code/rd vᵢ[pcᵢ−1])`;\n643\t   `Wᵢ = (zipmap (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) slice(fᵢ))`; `Eᵢ` = §4.4\n644\t   from `fᵢ.frames` and `fᵢ.store-of`.\n645\t5. pending from the engine fields.\n646\t\n647\t**Lift, `tail`.** From a wait entry whose `pc−1` in H is a tail `:call`:\n648\t`A` and `site = h->a(pcₕ − 1)`; `K` as in 4; pending; the entry's\n649\t`frames`, `store-of` and `slice(current)` are **discarded** (dead\n650\tlayout syntax; frozen §2.4).\n651\t\n652\t**Lower, `act`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:pc = off + a->h(pc−1) + 1`;\n653\t`:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of` from `E`; `:stack` = the\n654\tconcatenation over `K` outermost first of each `slice(fᵢ)` =\n655\t`(mapv Wᵢ (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)))`, then `slice(current) =\n656\t(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`; `:continuation` = for each `fᵢ`\n657\t`{:return-pc (+ offᵢ (a->h_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) 1) :frames (§4.4 from Eᵢ)\n658\t:stack-base bᵢ :store-of …}` where **`bᵢ` is the cumulative length of\n659\tthe slices up to and including `slice(fᵢ)`**: `b₀ = |slice(f₀)|`, `bᵢ =\n660\tb_{i−1} + |slice(fᵢ)|`. This is the inverse of the lift's\n661\t`slice(fᵢ) = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)`: the kernel's base is the caller's\n662\tresidual height, which is exactly the end of the caller's own slice\n663\t(`stack.cljc:731-733`). On the multi-frame fixture of §8.3 item 6\n664\t(five return frames, slices `[0,0) [0,0) [0,0) [0,2) [2,4)`) this emits\n665\tbases `0, 0, 0, 2, 4`; the before-slice rule would emit `0, 0, 0, 0, 2`\n666\tand the kernel would truncate to the wrong height on return. `:format\n667\tstack/format-tag`; `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's. `stack-restore`\n668\tthen conjes the delivered value onto `:stack` at `:pc`, the `:push`-free\n669\tposition after the parking instruction, exactly where H expects the\n670\tresult.\n671\t\n672\t**Lower, `tail`, and the tail-completion seam.** Today the stack kernel\n673\tresumes a parked host effect at the entry's `pc` by pushing the value\n674\t(`stack-restore`, `:566`); for a **tail** call that resumes the body at\n675\t`a->h(site)+1`, which then runs the layout syntax after the tail call\n676\t(for a tail call in operand position: the remaining pushes and the\n677\tenclosing call) **before** reaching `:return`. That is not the canonical\n678\tthrough-return delivery (frozen §2.4, §4.1 \"effectful call, tail\":\n679\tdelivery **through return** against `K`). The layout therefore cannot\n680\tlower a canonical `tail` into today's stack kernel. **PROPOSED, gated\n681\t(§8.3 slice 6c-pre):** the stack kernel gains a tail-completion seam:\n682\tits park-entry builders mark an entry whose parking instruction is a\n683\ttail `:call` with `:resume-mode :return-result` (the register kernel's\n684\texisting vocabulary, `register.cljc:458-468`), and `stack-restore`\n685\tdelivers such an entry by the **return transition** (pop the top frame,\n686\ttruncate `:stack` to its base, push the value, restore its `frames`,\n687\t`store-of` and `return-pc`; halt with the value when `K` is empty) instead\n688\tof resuming at `pc`; the retained-FFI re-park and the response decoding\n689\t(`stack-restore`'s `:request-sent` and `:call-id` steps, `:548-557`) keep\n690\ttheir order before the final delivery, as in the register kernel.\n691\tLowering a canonical `tail` then produces\n692\t`{:image row-of-A :pc (+ off (a->h site) 1) :resume-mode :return-result\n693\t:stack (concat of the K slices, in order) :frames [] :store-of nil\n694\t:continuation …}` with each continuation frame's `:stack-base bᵢ` the\n695\t**same cumulative-including rule as the `act` lowering** (the last\n696\tframe's base equals the stack's length, since the current activation\n697\tcontributes no slice); the `:frames`/`:store-of` values are unobservable\n698\t(the return transition replaces them) and the harness checks that (§5.6\n699\titem 8). File:\n700\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc` (not a 4b or phase-5 file); the\n701\tregister kernel needs no change.\n702\t\n703\t### 4.4 Positional lexical frames, the free environment and `E`\n704\t\n705\t**DECIDED** by frozen §2.1 and §2.4 (`E_c[ps ↦ a]` by `bind-params`),\n706\tphase-5 §1.2 (the owner chain), and the kernels' resolution of free\n707\tnames through the task-wide `free-env` (`stack.cljc:683`:\n708\t`(engine/resolve-var free-env …)`). Both de Bruijn kernels hold the\n709\tlexical environment as positional frames `F = [[V …] …]` outermost first,\n710\tone per enclosing closure activation, plus the task-wide `free-env`.\n711\t\n712\t**Forward (F → E), lossless.** At a pc of body `b` with\n713\t`chain = [c₀ … c_{j}]` outermost first (phase-5's `chain` reversed):\n714\t\n715\t```\n716\tE = (merge free-env\n717\t           (bind-params params(c₀) F[0])\n718\t           …\n719\t           (bind-params params(c_j) F[j]))\n720\t    ∪ {engine/store-of-key M}  when the activation's closure carries store-of M\n721\t```\n722\t\n723\tThis reproduces the register evaluator's `E` on closure entry\n724\t(`semantic_register.cljc:141-143`: `(merge (:env c) (bind-params\n725\t(:params c) args))`, `(:env c)` being the creating activation's `E`,\n726\titself `free-env` merged with the outer chain), including rightmost-wins\n727\tduplicates, nil-fill and extra-argument dropping, which are\n728\t`engine/bind-params`'s.\n729\t\n730\t**Inverse (E → F), and why the free environment travels.** The reviewer's\n731\tcounterexample, with initial free `x = 99`:\n732\t\n733\t```clojure\n734\t(let [f (fn [] x)]\n735\t  ((fn [x] (f (stream-next c))) 1))\n736\t```\n737\t\n738\tAt the blocked read the current `E` binds `x` to 1 (the parameter). The\n739\tearlier revision recovered the receiver's free environment as \"`E` minus\n740\tthe chain's names\", which drops `x` entirely, and the lowered de Bruijn\n741\tclosure `f`, whose body is `[:load-free x]`, resolves `x` through the\n742\ttask-wide `free-env` and loses 99. **So the task's free environment is\n743\tcarried explicitly** (root slot `free-env`, §2.6; §6.3), every `E` is\n744\tchecked against it off the lexical names (rule E2), and the inverse is:\n745\t\n746\t```\n747\tfree-env  := the root's free-env row (never derived from any E)\n748\tF[i]      := (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))      for i = 0 … j\n749\t```\n750\t\n751\t`F[i]` for an **outer** frame whose parameter is shadowed by an inner\n752\tframe receives the inner value, not the outer one; that value is\n753\tunobservable from `body(pc)` (resolution picks the innermost binder,\n754\t`resolve-name`, `debruijn.cljc:617-637`), and the canonical frame does\n755\tnot carry it (frozen §2.1: parameters live in `E`, and `E` holds one\n756\tvalue per name). The round-trip law is therefore stated on **canonical\n757\tframes** (`lift(lower(frame)) = frame`), not on kernel state, and the\n758\tharness's `E` check (§5.6 item 6) compares the layout's `E` with the\n759\tevaluator's; shadowed outer positional values are not a claim of this\n760\tdesign. The free bindings that *are* observable, by any closure's\n761\t`:load-free`, are preserved exactly by the root slot.\n762\t\n763\t**Closure values.** A de Bruijn closure (positional marker: segment,\n764\tentry, arity, frames `F`, store-of) reconstructs to the canonical\n765\tclosure (`{:type :closure :params :entry :segment :env}`, evaluator\n766\t§1.8): `params` from A's `:closure` tuple at the owner pc of the entry\n767\tbody, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's captured `F` and the\n768\ttask's `free-env`, `entry` the body pc. The inverse reads `F` back from\n769\t`env` by the chain. Checked both ways (§5.6 item 7).\n770\t\n771\t### 4.5 What a layout never does\n772\t\n773\t**DECIDED.** A layout reads a kernel's state, A and the carried task\n774\tcontext; it never runs code, never fills a binding from the receiver's\n775\tstore or free environment (the free environment is the **carried** one,\n776\t§6.3), never reads a native image's hash as an identity, and never\n777\tinvents a safepoint: a kernel pc whose predecessor is not a parking\n778\tinstruction is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2).\n779\t\n780\t---\n781\t\n782\t## 5. The safepoint harness\n783\t\n784\t### 5.1 What it proves\n785\t\n786\t**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 6 gate; UCF §7.11). For every row of the\n787\texpanded matrix, both delivery modes, nested and reified captures,\n788\tmodule contexts and every refusal: lift at the safepoint, lower into a"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0da6a8ed-da80-4f95-b938-953b362e5554","uuid":"d294cafd-3f76-4a0f-8ac6-16403a25cabb","timestamp":"2026-10-12T18:26:21.850Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"## 4. Layouts: reconstructing the canonical frame\n\n### 4.1 What a layout is\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.3; UCF §7.4.2). A foreign engine publishes a\nstatic map per segment and engine (`A`, resume pcs, `def`/`use`/`L`\nfrom `analysis/analyze`, `lexically-required`, and a layout from\nphysical location to virtual register) and reconstructs the canonical\nframe from its state at a safepoint; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` on\nadmitted canonical frames. Two kernels are intended to reconstruct\ncanonical frames: the de Bruijn register kernel over `R(A)` and the de\nBruijn stack kernel over `H(A)` (phase 5). The AST walker is not (frozen\n§10 phase 6 names the three kernels; the walker has no pc).\n\n**PROPOSED** namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout`, file\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, requiring\n`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `.derive` (phase 5),\n`yin.vm.debruijn` (`resolve-name`), `yin.vm.engine` (`bind-params`), and\nthe two kernels' public state. Public: `register-layout`,\n`stack-layout` (static, per segment), `lift-act`, `lift-tail`,\n`lower-act`, `lower-tail` (per kernel, dispatching on the kernel's\n`link-format`).\n\n### 4.2 The register kernel's layout\n\n**DECIDED** by phase-5 §2.4 (R's bodies and pcs are A's) and §2.3\n(`R(v) = locals + slot(v)`); the kernel's payload shape from\n`debruijn/register.cljc:690-704` (call frame: `:site-pc :return-pc\n:frames :regs :live :dest :store-of`) and `write-back` (`:455-485`:\n`:write-result` rebuilds a dense register vector from `regs` and writes\n`dest`; `:return-result` runs the return transition).\n\n**Static half.** For segment `A` with `R = (:image (derive/register-image\nv))`: for every pc `p`, `v[p]` names virtual registers in its `rd`,\n`:reg` and `:regs` slots and `R[p]` names physical registers in the same\nslots. `phys(body, v)` is read off any pc that names `v`; it is total on\nthe virtuals a body names. Its inverse is **restricted**: `virt(p, s)`\nis defined only for `s` in the physical image of\n`live-out*(p) = ⋃ L(q) for q ∈ (analysis/successors v p)` (the actual\nsuccessors, phase-5 §2.5; empty for a tail call) at a **reachable**\nboundary pc `p` (`analysis/reachable`); there it is \"the `v ∈ live-out*(p)`\nwith `phys(v) = s`\", single-valued because the allocator's intervals\ncover every live point and distinct live virtuals hold distinct slots\n(a property the harness checks, §5.6 item 3, never assumed). Physical\noperands of **unreachable** layout-syntax instructions (the structural\nslot shared by several definition-less ids, phase-5 §2.2) are not\ninverted; they are validated separately by the kernel's own\n`register-image-defect` and by phase-5's gates, and never reach a\ncanonical frame (no reachable instruction reads them).\n\n**Lift, `act`.** From the kernel's wait entry with `:resume-mode\n:write-result` (payload `:image :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live\n:continuation :dest :store-of`, v2 amendment §5.3):\n\n1. `A` = the A the image row of the entry's `:image` was derived from\n   (the kernel's code space is keyed by A after phase 5; R is its\n   checksum).\n2. relative `pc` = the entry's `pc` minus the image row's offset\n   (`image-row`/`absolute-pc`, `debruijn/register.cljc:326-337`); R's pcs\n   are A's, so this is the canonical pc.\n3. `rd = (code/rd (nth v (dec pc)))` **from A**, never the physical\n   `:dest` (which is `phys(rd)`).\n4. `W = {virt(pc−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ regs}`; the kernel's `live` is\n   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(pc−1) rd)))` (the subtraction is on\n   **virtual** ids before mapping: on the worked example `live-out*(3) =\n   L(4) = {1 2}`, `(disj … 2) = {1}`, `phys → [0]`; subtracting the\n   physical `3` from `{1 2}` would wrongly keep `2`), so `(keys W) =\n   saved(pc, rd)`, which rule A5/A6 then checks.\n5. `E` = §4.4 from the entry's `frames` and `store-of`, at `body(pc)`.\n6. `K`: for each continuation frame `f`, **independently**: its\n   `return-pc` is absolute in the kernel's concatenated code space, so\n   first its image row `rowₖ = (image-row images return-pc)` and relative\n   `pcₖ = return-pc − offsetₖ`, then `Aₖ` of that row (frames may cross\n   images: a module closure called from another image returns into its\n   caller's image), `rdₖ = (code/rd (nth vₖ (dec pcₖ)))` from `Aₖ`, never\n   `f.dest`; `Wₖ = {virt_{Aₖ}(pcₖ−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ f.regs}`; `Eₖ` = §4.4\n   from `f.frames` and `f.store-of` at `body(pcₖ)` in `Aₖ`.\n7. pending: the engine fields of the entry, as `lift-pending!` maps them\n   (`handoff.cljc:674-749`).\n\n**Lift, `tail`.** From an entry with `:resume-mode :return-result`\n(`dest nil`, `live []`): `A` and the relative `site = site-pc` as in 1-2\n(the entry's `site-pc` is the tail call; its `pc` is `site+1`); `K` as\nin 6; pending as in 7; **no** `W`, `E` or `pc` are read: the entry's\n`regs` (empty by `live []`) and `frames` are discarded (frozen §2.4: a\ntail state carries no window and no `E`).\n\n**Lower, `act`.** Given a canonical `act` and the receiver's attached\nimage for `A` (derived by `derive/register-image` from the carried\nvector, offset `off`): `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + pc − 1`;\n`:pc = off + pc`; `:regs = (sort-by first [[phys(v) V] for [v V] in W])`;\n`:live = (sort (map phys (keys W)))` (the kernel's `live-exact` rule\nrecomputes `body-liveness` and must agree); `:dest = phys(rd)`;\n`:resume-mode :write-result`; `:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of`\nfrom `E`; `:continuation` = for each `K[i]`, with its own `Aₖ`'s image\nrow `offₖ`: `{:site-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ −1) :return-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ) :regs\n… :live … :dest (phys_{Aₖ} rdₖ) :frames (§4.4 from Eₖ) :store-of …}`;\n`:format reffects/format-tag`, `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's current\naggregate (never carried). Engine fields from the pending\n(`lower-frame!`, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`), `:deliver` carried.\n\n**Lower, `tail`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + site`;\n`:pc = off + site + 1`; `:regs []`; `:live []`; `:dest nil`;\n`:resume-mode :return-result`; `:frames []` and `:store-of nil`\n(unused: `write-back`'s `:return-result` branch reads `:frames` and\n`:store-of` only to assign them before the return transition replaces\nthem from the popped frame, `register.cljc:461-468`; the harness checks\nthat the values chosen here are never observable, §5.6 item 8);\n`:continuation` as for `act`. The register kernel already supports this\ndelivery (`:return-result`, `register.cljc:458-468`).\n\n### 4.3 The stack kernel's layout\n\n**DECIDED** by phase-5 §3.2 (the emitter's walk) and the kernel's call\nand return arms (`debruijn/stack.cljc:716-776`): a non-tail `:call`\npops `f` and the `argc` operands, leaving `stack'`, and pushes\n`{:return-pc (inc pc) :frames frames :stack-base (count stack')}`\n(`:728-733`); `:return` truncates to the frame's `stack-base` and pushes\nthe value (`:766-772`); a host-function effect parks with the\ncontinuation after the call site and `stack-restore` resumes by conjing\nthe value at the entry's `pc` (`:748-752`, `:555-568`).\n\n**Static half.**\n\n1. **pc correspondence.** The stack emitter visits parse nodes in\n   `project`'s order and emits one instruction per A instruction plus a\n   `:push` after each child of a call, each `:stream-put` target and\n   each `:ffi-call` operand (phase-5 §3.2). So H **with every `:push`\n   removed** corresponds one-to-one, in order, with A: the k-th non-push\n   H instruction ↔ A pc `k`; `h->a`, `a->h` are the two directions. The\n   harness checks mnemonic families agree at every k (§5.6 item 4).\n2. **operand layout.** For an A pc `p` and the parse node at `p`,\n   `stack-layout(p)` is the vector, bottom to top, of the virtual\n   registers whose values are pushed and not yet consumed while control\n   is inside `p`'s expression: for each ancestor `n` and the child `c` on\n   the path to `p`, the children of `n` before `c` that `n` pushes (a\n   `:call`: the operator and the operands before `c`; a `:stream-put`:\n   the target when `c` is the value; an `:ffi-call`: the operands before\n   `c`; `:if`, `:define`, `:stream-cursor/next/close`, `:resume`: none).\n   **Checked** (§5.6 item 5): for a **non-tail** parking instruction,\n   `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p) (code/rd v[p]))`.\n   For a **tail** `:call` the canonical state has no window and\n   `stack-layout(p)` is not consulted (it may be non-empty for a tail\n   call in operand position, whose pushed siblings are dead layout\n   syntax).\n\n**K slices (corrected).** Let the kernel's continuation be\n`[f₀ … f_{m−1}]` outermost first with bases `b₀ … b_{m−1}` and the\noperand stack `St` of length `n`. Because `bᵢ = (count stack')` is the\ncaller's residual height **after** popping the callee's operands, the\nslice of operands belonging to activation `i` is\n\n```\nslice(f₀)      = St[0, b₀)\nslice(fᵢ)      = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)        for 0 < i < m\nslice(current) = St[b_{m−1}, n)         ; or St[0, n) when K is empty\n```\n\n(the review's correction: `[previous-base, base_i)` with previous-base\n0, and the current activation `[last-base, count)`).\n\n**Lift, `act`.** From a wait entry `{:image :pc :frames :stack\n:continuation :store-of}` (v2 amendment §5.2) whose `pc−1` in H is **not**\na tail `:call`:\n\n1. `A` from the image row; relative H pc `pcₕ = pc − off`; canonical\n   `pc = h->a(pcₕ − 1) + 1`; `rd = (code/rd v[pc−1])`.\n2. `W = (zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice(current))`; the two have\n   equal length (checked) and `(keys W) = saved(pc, rd)` (rule A5/A6).\n3. `E` = §4.4 from `frames` and `store-of` at `body(pc)`.\n4. `K`: for `fᵢ` **independently**: its `return-pc` is absolute; row and\n   relative `pcₕᵢ` through the stack kernel's own offset table\n   (`debruijn/stack.cljc:355`, `absolute-pc`'s inverse); `Aᵢ` of that row;\n   `pcᵢ = h->a_{Aᵢ}(pcₕᵢ − 1) + 1`; `rdᵢ = (code/rd vᵢ[pcᵢ−1])`;\n   `Wᵢ = (zipmap (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) slice(fᵢ))`; `Eᵢ` = §4.4\n   from `fᵢ.frames` and `fᵢ.store-of`.\n5. pending from the engine fields.\n\n**Lift, `tail`.** From a wait entry whose `pc−1` in H is a tail `:call`:\n`A` and `site = h->a(pcₕ − 1)`; `K` as in 4; pending; the entry's\n`frames`, `store-of` and `slice(current)` are **discarded** (dead\nlayout syntax; frozen §2.4).\n\n**Lower, `act`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:pc = off + a->h(pc−1) + 1`;\n`:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of` from `E`; `:stack` = the\nconcatenation over `K` outermost first of each `slice(fᵢ)` =\n`(mapv Wᵢ (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)))`, then `slice(current) =\n(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`; `:continuation` = for each `fᵢ`\n`{:return-pc (+ offᵢ (a->h_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) 1) :frames (§4.4 from Eᵢ)\n:stack-base bᵢ :store-of …}` where **`bᵢ` is the cumulative length of\nthe slices up to and including `slice(fᵢ)`**: `b₀ = |slice(f₀)|`, `bᵢ =\nb_{i−1} + |slice(fᵢ)|`. This is the inverse of the lift's\n`slice(fᵢ) = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)`: the kernel's base is the caller's\nresidual height, which is exactly the end of the caller's own slice\n(`stack.cljc:731-733`). On the multi-frame fixture of §8.3 item 6\n(five return frames, slices `[0,0) [0,0) [0,0) [0,2) [2,4)`) this emits\nbases `0, 0, 0, 2, 4`; the before-slice rule would emit `0, 0, 0, 0, 2`\nand the kernel would truncate to the wrong height on return. `:format\nstack/format-tag`; `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's. `stack-restore`\nthen conjes the delivered value onto `:stack` at `:pc`, the `:push`-free\nposition after the parking instruction, exactly where H expects the\nresult.\n\n**Lower, `tail`, and the tail-completion seam.** Today the stack kernel\nresumes a parked host effect at the entry's `pc` by pushing the value\n(`stack-restore`, `:566`); for a **tail** call that resumes the body at\n`a->h(site)+1`, which then runs the layout syntax after the tail call\n(for a tail call in operand position: the remaining pushes and the\nenclosing call) **before** reaching `:return`. That is not the canonical\nthrough-return delivery (frozen §2.4, §4.1 \"effectful call, tail\":\ndelivery **through return** against `K`). The layout therefore cannot\nlower a canonical `tail` into today's stack kernel. **PROPOSED, gated\n(§8.3 slice 6c-pre):** the stack kernel gains a tail-completion seam:\nits park-entry builders mark an entry whose parking instruction is a\ntail `:call` with `:resume-mode :return-result` (the register kernel's\nexisting vocabulary, `register.cljc:458-468`), and `stack-restore`\ndelivers such an entry by the **return transition** (pop the top frame,\ntruncate `:stack` to its base, push the value, restore its `frames`,\n`store-of` and `return-pc`; halt with the value when `K` is empty) instead\nof resuming at `pc`; the retained-FFI re-park and the response decoding\n(`stack-restore`'s `:request-sent` and `:call-id` steps, `:548-557`) keep\ntheir order before the final delivery, as in the register kernel.\nLowering a canonical `tail` then produces\n`{:image row-of-A :pc (+ off (a->h site) 1) :resume-mode :return-result\n:stack (concat of the K slices, in order) :frames [] :store-of nil\n:continuation …}` with each continuation frame's `:stack-base bᵢ` the\n**same cumulative-including rule as the `act` lowering** (the last\nframe's base equals the stack's length, since the current activation\ncontributes no slice); the `:frames`/`:store-of` values are unobservable\n(the return transition replaces them) and the harness checks that (§5.6\nitem 8). File:\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc` (not a 4b or phase-5 file); the\nregister kernel needs no change.\n\n### 4.4 Positional lexical frames, the free environment and `E`\n\n**DECIDED** by frozen §2.1 and §2.4 (`E_c[ps ↦ a]` by `bind-params`),\nphase-5 §1.2 (the owner chain), and the kernels' resolution of free\nnames through the task-wide `free-env` (`stack.cljc:683`:\n`(engine/resolve-var free-env …)`). Both de Bruijn kernels hold the\nlexical environment as positional frames `F = [[V …] …]` outermost first,\none per enclosing closure activation, plus the task-wide `free-env`.\n\n**Forward (F → E), lossless.** At a pc of body `b` with\n`chain = [c₀ … c_{j}]` outermost first (phase-5's `chain` reversed):\n\n```\nE = (merge free-env\n           (bind-params params(c₀) F[0])\n           …\n           (bind-params params(c_j) F[j]))\n    ∪ {engine/store-of-key M}  when the activation's closure carries store-of M\n```\n\nThis reproduces the register evaluator's `E` on closure entry\n(`semantic_register.cljc:141-143`: `(merge (:env c) (bind-params\n(:params c) args))`, `(:env c)` being the creating activation's `E`,\nitself `free-env` merged with the outer chain), including rightmost-wins\nduplicates, nil-fill and extra-argument dropping, which are\n`engine/bind-params`'s.\n\n**Inverse (E → F), and why the free environment travels.** The reviewer's\ncounterexample, with initial free `x = 99`:\n\n```clojure\n(let [f (fn [] x)]\n  ((fn [x] (f (stream-next c))) 1))\n```\n\nAt the blocked read the current `E` binds `x` to 1 (the parameter). The\nearlier revision recovered the receiver's free environment as \"`E` minus\nthe chain's names\", which drops `x` entirely, and the lowered de Bruijn\nclosure `f`, whose body is `[:load-free x]`, resolves `x` through the\ntask-wide `free-env` and loses 99. **So the task's free environment is\ncarried explicitly** (root slot `free-env`, §2.6; §6.3), every `E` is\nchecked against it off the lexical names (rule E2), and the inverse is:\n\n```\nfree-env  := the root's free-env row (never derived from any E)\nF[i]      := (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))      for i = 0 … j\n```\n\n`F[i]` for an **outer** frame whose parameter is shadowed by an inner\nframe receives the inner value, not the outer one; that value is\nunobservable from `body(pc)` (resolution picks the innermost binder,\n`resolve-name`, `debruijn.cljc:617-637`), and the canonical frame does\nnot carry it (frozen §2.1: parameters live in `E`, and `E` holds one\nvalue per name). The round-trip law is therefore stated on **canonical\nframes** (`lift(lower(frame)) = frame`), not on kernel state, and the\nharness's `E` check (§5.6 item 6) compares the layout's `E` with the\nevaluator's; shadowed outer positional values are not a claim of this\ndesign. The free bindings that *are* observable, by any closure's\n`:load-free`, are preserved exactly by the root slot.\n\n**Closure values.** A de Bruijn closure (positional marker: segment,\nentry, arity, frames `F`, store-of) reconstructs to the canonical\nclosure (`{:type :closure :params :entry :segment :env}`, evaluator\n§1.8): `params` from A's `:closure` tuple at the owner pc of the entry\nbody, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's captured `F` and the\ntask's `free-env`, `entry` the body pc. The inverse reads `F` back from\n`env` by the chain. Checked both ways (§5.6 item 7).\n\n### 4.5 What a layout never does\n\n**DECIDED.** A layout reads a kernel's state, A and the carried task\ncontext; it never runs code, never fills a binding from the receiver's\nstore or free environment (the free environment is the **carried** one,\n§6.3), never reads a native image's hash as an identity, and never\ninvents a safepoint: a kernel pc whose predecessor is not a parking\ninstruction is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2).\n\n---\n\n## 5. The safepoint harness\n\n### 5.1 What it proves\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 6 gate; UCF §7.11). For every row of the\nexpanded matrix, both delivery modes, nested and reified captures,\nmodule contexts and every refusal: lift at the safepoint, lower into a","numLines":320,"startLine":469,"totalLines":1762}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Jv3mG2ivzpNNwPp1vdNoTS","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
