{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WDEEK4ddLhvot2HgEgiuCG","type":"tool_result","content":"1261\t### 8.3 Slice 6c: the layouts, the stack seam, the cross-engine lanes\n1262\t\n1263\t**6c-pre, the stack tail-completion seam.** File to change:\n1264\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc` (§4.3): park-entry builders mark a\n1265\ttail-call park `:resume-mode :return-result`; `stack-restore` delivers\n1266\tsuch an entry by the return transition. Test to create:\n1267\t`test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_tail_completion_test.cljc`: a tail\n1268\teffectful call parked and resumed delivers through `K` (pops the frame,\n1269\ttruncates to its base, pushes the value) and halts with the value when\n1270\t`K` is empty; a tail call in operand position never executes the layout\n1271\tsyntax after it; non-tail parks are unchanged (the old stack tests stay\n1272\tgreen). **Depends on** nothing of 4b or 5.\n1273\t\n1274\t**6c proper.** Files to create: `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`\n1275\t(§4); `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc`; the two extra\n1276\tlanes added to the 6b harness file. **Depends on:** 5a, 5b (`derive`),\n1277\t6b, 6c-pre.\n1278\t\n1279\t**Acceptance (6c).**\n1280\t\n1281\t1. `layout-test/register-pairing`: for every `P ∈ C`, `phys` is total on\n1282\t   every body's named virtuals; `virt(p, s)` is single-valued at every\n1283\t   reachable boundary pc (§5.6 item 3).\n1284\t2. `layout-test/register-live-is-live-out`: §5.6 item 3's second clause,\n1285\t   tail boundaries `[]`.\n1286\t3. `layout-test/register-rd-from-a`: on `:worked-example`'s register\n1287\t   lane at the inner call's park, the canonical `rd` is `2` while the\n1288\t   kernel's `dest` is `3`; the K frame of a nested non-tail call in\n1289\t   `:depth-3` has its `rd` from A, not its physical `dest`.\n1290\t4. `layout-test/stack-correspondence`: §5.6 item 4.\n1291\t5. `layout-test/stack-layout-is-the-saved-set`: §5.6 item 5 and the\n1292\t   worked cases: `(f (g x) y)` at the inner call → `[1]`; `(f (if c (g)\n1293\t   2) y)` at `(g)` → `[1]`.\n1294\t6. `layout-test/stack-slices-partition`: §5.6 item 9; and the\n1295\t   multi-frame fixture, the expression `(cat [1] ((fn [x] (cat [2] ((fn\n1296\t   [y] (cat [3] (next-of c))) 2))) 1))` under the harness's standard\n1297\t   binding setup: `with-cat` outermost, then `(let1 s (stream/make 4)\n1298\t   (let1 c (cursor-of s) …))` around it (the stream is made inside the\n1299\t   program, as every row does: that is this fixture's chosen setup. A\n1300\t   primitive `cat` supplied in the free environment would sit in a live\n1301\t   slot and is no carrier value, `:continuation-registers`,\n1302\t   `debruijn_register_effects.cljc:196`; an authentic cursor reference is\n1303\t   machine data with an explicit carrier marker, `engine.cljc:899-905`, so\n1304\t   in-program construction of `c` is a fixture choice, not a carrier\n1305\t   restriction). **Derivation of the expression\n1306\t   alone** (`cat` and `c` taken as given): the outer application of\n1307\t   `(fn [x] …)` pops its closure and `1`, leaving the outer `cat` and\n1308\t   `[1]` pushed, so its frame's base is `2`; the inner application leaves\n1309\t   the middle `cat` and `[2]` as well, base `4`; the current activation\n1310\t   has pushed its `cat` and `[3]` and the read has popped its cursor\n1311\t   operand (`stack.cljc:839`), so the operand stack has six values:\n1312\t   slices `[0,2)` = `[cat [1]]`, `[2,4)` = `[cat [2]]`, current `[4,6)` =\n1313\t   `[cat [3]]`, each slice's `stack-layout` being `[operator-rd\n1314\t   literal-rd]` of its `cat` call and equal to that activation's saved\n1315\t   window. **As run**, the three binding applications each pop their\n1316\t   closure and single operand off an otherwise empty stack, so `K` has\n1317\t   **five** frames with bases `0, 0, 0, 2, 4`, slices `[0,0) [0,0) [0,0)\n1318\t   [0,2) [2,4)`, current `[4,6)`, stack length six; the test pins these\n1319\t   bases from the kernel's state, checks the partition, and checks that\n1320\t   lowering the lifted frame emits the same five bases (§4.3; the\n1321\t   before-slice rule would emit `0, 0, 0, 0, 2`).\n1322\t7. `layout-test/env-reconstruction`: §5.6 item 6.\n1323\t8. `layout-test/closures-round-trip`: §5.6 item 7.\n1324\t9. `layout-test/tail-lowering-is-unobservable`: §5.6 item 8.\n1325\t10. `harness/cross-engine-canonical-equality`: law 4 for every row ×\n1326\t    segment under `canonical-under`.\n1327\t11. `harness/cross-engine-lowering`: law 5, six ordered pairs.\n1328\t12. `harness/layout-refusals`: `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` from a kernel\n1329\t    stepped to a non-safepoint pc; a register entry whose `live` was\n1330\t    tampered reconstructs a frame the validator refuses (`:window-extra`\n1331\t    or `:window-missing` by the tamper's direction).\n1332\t13. kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts.\n1333\t\n1334\t### 8.4 Phase 7's entry conditions\n1335\t\n1336\t**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 7). 6a-6c green on three hosts; the row\n1337\tcarrier the only writer and reader the semantic-register kernel has; a\n1338\tdocumented seam for the holder, checkpoint, ledger and authority paths\n1339\t(phase 7 wires it; phase 6 edits none of them); the Stage D/E scenarios\n1340\t(`stage_e_scenarios.cljc`, `checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`) listed with, for\n1341\teach, the §5.2 row that exercises its parking shape; the export-recovery\n1342\tobject (UCF §7.7.4, format version 1 over the old codec) re-based on the\n1343\trow carrier by phase 7, since it embeds a body; and the free-environment\n1344\tretention seam (§6.3) either landed by the 4b track or the explicit\n1345\t`:free-env` argument threaded through the holder's export path.\n1346\t\n1347\t### 8.5 Ordering and concurrency\n1348\t\n1349\t| | needs | concurrent with |\n1350\t|---|---|---|\n1351\t| 6a | 4b stage 1 (branch) | 4b stage 2, 5a-5c, 6c-pre |\n1352\t| 6c-pre | nothing of 4b or 5 | everything |\n1353\t| 6b | 4b stage 2; 5c (scanners) | 5a, 5b, 6c-pre |\n1354\t| 6c | 5a, 5b, 6b, 6c-pre | 5c |\n1355\t\n1356\tNo file of 4b or phase 5 is edited. The old kernel file\n1357\t`debruijn/stack.cljc` is edited by 6c-pre only, for the seam; the\n1358\tregister kernel is not edited. `handoff.cljc` and the old harness are\n1359\tnot edited.\n1360\t\n1361\t---\n1362\t\n1363\t## 9. Verification honesty\n1364\t\n1365\t- **Hand-derived numbers.** `saved(4, 2) = {1}` and `L(5)` on the worked\n1366\t  example are the phase-3 tests' (`analysis_test.cljc:131-139`). The\n1367\t  stack slices `[f]` at the inner call of `(f (g x) y)` and at `(g)` in\n1368\t  `(f (if c (g) 2) y)` were derived twice from the §4.3 walk and agreed\n1369\t  with the reviewer's derivation; the register `phys(1) = 0`, `phys(2) =\n1370\t  3` follow from phase-5 §6.3 row 2, whose pinned `live` at pc 3 is `[0]`"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0da6a8ed-da80-4f95-b938-953b362e5554","uuid":"8456f01c-6b08-48a1-959f-8ce714baaea6","timestamp":"2026-10-12T18:26:24.594Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"### 8.3 Slice 6c: the layouts, the stack seam, the cross-engine lanes\n\n**6c-pre, the stack tail-completion seam.** File to change:\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc` (§4.3): park-entry builders mark a\ntail-call park `:resume-mode :return-result`; `stack-restore` delivers\nsuch an entry by the return transition. Test to create:\n`test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_tail_completion_test.cljc`: a tail\neffectful call parked and resumed delivers through `K` (pops the frame,\ntruncates to its base, pushes the value) and halts with the value when\n`K` is empty; a tail call in operand position never executes the layout\nsyntax after it; non-tail parks are unchanged (the old stack tests stay\ngreen). **Depends on** nothing of 4b or 5.\n\n**6c proper.** Files to create: `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`\n(§4); `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc`; the two extra\nlanes added to the 6b harness file. **Depends on:** 5a, 5b (`derive`),\n6b, 6c-pre.\n\n**Acceptance (6c).**\n\n1. `layout-test/register-pairing`: for every `P ∈ C`, `phys` is total on\n   every body's named virtuals; `virt(p, s)` is single-valued at every\n   reachable boundary pc (§5.6 item 3).\n2. `layout-test/register-live-is-live-out`: §5.6 item 3's second clause,\n   tail boundaries `[]`.\n3. `layout-test/register-rd-from-a`: on `:worked-example`'s register\n   lane at the inner call's park, the canonical `rd` is `2` while the\n   kernel's `dest` is `3`; the K frame of a nested non-tail call in\n   `:depth-3` has its `rd` from A, not its physical `dest`.\n4. `layout-test/stack-correspondence`: §5.6 item 4.\n5. `layout-test/stack-layout-is-the-saved-set`: §5.6 item 5 and the\n   worked cases: `(f (g x) y)` at the inner call → `[1]`; `(f (if c (g)\n   2) y)` at `(g)` → `[1]`.\n6. `layout-test/stack-slices-partition`: §5.6 item 9; and the\n   multi-frame fixture, the expression `(cat [1] ((fn [x] (cat [2] ((fn\n   [y] (cat [3] (next-of c))) 2))) 1))` under the harness's standard\n   binding setup: `with-cat` outermost, then `(let1 s (stream/make 4)\n   (let1 c (cursor-of s) …))` around it (the stream is made inside the\n   program, as every row does: that is this fixture's chosen setup. A\n   primitive `cat` supplied in the free environment would sit in a live\n   slot and is no carrier value, `:continuation-registers`,\n   `debruijn_register_effects.cljc:196`; an authentic cursor reference is\n   machine data with an explicit carrier marker, `engine.cljc:899-905`, so\n   in-program construction of `c` is a fixture choice, not a carrier\n   restriction). **Derivation of the expression\n   alone** (`cat` and `c` taken as given): the outer application of\n   `(fn [x] …)` pops its closure and `1`, leaving the outer `cat` and\n   `[1]` pushed, so its frame's base is `2`; the inner application leaves\n   the middle `cat` and `[2]` as well, base `4`; the current activation\n   has pushed its `cat` and `[3]` and the read has popped its cursor\n   operand (`stack.cljc:839`), so the operand stack has six values:\n   slices `[0,2)` = `[cat [1]]`, `[2,4)` = `[cat [2]]`, current `[4,6)` =\n   `[cat [3]]`, each slice's `stack-layout` being `[operator-rd\n   literal-rd]` of its `cat` call and equal to that activation's saved\n   window. **As run**, the three binding applications each pop their\n   closure and single operand off an otherwise empty stack, so `K` has\n   **five** frames with bases `0, 0, 0, 2, 4`, slices `[0,0) [0,0) [0,0)\n   [0,2) [2,4)`, current `[4,6)`, stack length six; the test pins these\n   bases from the kernel's state, checks the partition, and checks that\n   lowering the lifted frame emits the same five bases (§4.3; the\n   before-slice rule would emit `0, 0, 0, 0, 2`).\n7. `layout-test/env-reconstruction`: §5.6 item 6.\n8. `layout-test/closures-round-trip`: §5.6 item 7.\n9. `layout-test/tail-lowering-is-unobservable`: §5.6 item 8.\n10. `harness/cross-engine-canonical-equality`: law 4 for every row ×\n    segment under `canonical-under`.\n11. `harness/cross-engine-lowering`: law 5, six ordered pairs.\n12. `harness/layout-refusals`: `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` from a kernel\n    stepped to a non-safepoint pc; a register entry whose `live` was\n    tampered reconstructs a frame the validator refuses (`:window-extra`\n    or `:window-missing` by the tamper's direction).\n13. kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm` green on three hosts.\n\n### 8.4 Phase 7's entry conditions\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 7). 6a-6c green on three hosts; the row\ncarrier the only writer and reader the semantic-register kernel has; a\ndocumented seam for the holder, checkpoint, ledger and authority paths\n(phase 7 wires it; phase 6 edits none of them); the Stage D/E scenarios\n(`stage_e_scenarios.cljc`, `checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`) listed with, for\neach, the §5.2 row that exercises its parking shape; the export-recovery\nobject (UCF §7.7.4, format version 1 over the old codec) re-based on the\nrow carrier by phase 7, since it embeds a body; and the free-environment\nretention seam (§6.3) either landed by the 4b track or the explicit\n`:free-env` argument threaded through the holder's export path.\n\n### 8.5 Ordering and concurrency\n\n| | needs | concurrent with |\n|---|---|---|\n| 6a | 4b stage 1 (branch) | 4b stage 2, 5a-5c, 6c-pre |\n| 6c-pre | nothing of 4b or 5 | everything |\n| 6b | 4b stage 2; 5c (scanners) | 5a, 5b, 6c-pre |\n| 6c | 5a, 5b, 6b, 6c-pre | 5c |\n\nNo file of 4b or phase 5 is edited. The old kernel file\n`debruijn/stack.cljc` is edited by 6c-pre only, for the seam; the\nregister kernel is not edited. `handoff.cljc` and the old harness are\nnot edited.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Verification honesty\n\n- **Hand-derived numbers.** `saved(4, 2) = {1}` and `L(5)` on the worked\n  example are the phase-3 tests' (`analysis_test.cljc:131-139`). The\n  stack slices `[f]` at the inner call of `(f (g x) y)` and at `(g)` in\n  `(f (if c (g) 2) y)` were derived twice from the §4.3 walk and agreed\n  with the reviewer's derivation; the register `phys(1) = 0`, `phys(2) =\n  3` follow from phase-5 §6.3 row 2, whose pinned `live` at pc 3 is `[0]`","numLines":110,"startLine":1261,"totalLines":1762}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WDEEK4ddLhvot2HgEgiuCG","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
