{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016uDGdcX7vcKmjFJPvWzCb9","type":"tool_result","content":"540\t   grows, so an entry parked before an attach still names its row.\n541\t\n542\t   Then the FFI two-step, on the entry keys the `yin.vm.ffi` convention\n543\t   owns, in the semantic VM's placement:\n544\t\n545\t   - A `:request-sent` entry is a writer whose retained request has now been\n546\t     appended. It must not resume: its registers are re-parked as the\n547\t     call-out reader (`ffi/response-wait-entry`) and the machine stays\n548\t     blocked.\n549\t   - A `:call-id` entry is a response reader. The woken value is a response\n550\t     envelope, so `ffi/call-result` unwraps it and checks correlation, and\n551\t     the parked call leaves `:parked` rather than accumulating.\n552\t\n553\t   A `:return-result` entry delivers through the return transition.\n554\t   Otherwise the registers are written from the entry and `val` is conjed\n555\t   onto the restored `:stack` -- the B3 obligation that every\n556\t   value-producing opcode lands on the stack, since there is no\n557\t   accumulator. `:segment` is never written from the entry: the code space\n558\t   is kernel state, and since appending moves nothing, the entry's\n559\t   absolute pcs and stack bases still name what they named.\"\n560\t  [base entry val]\n561\t  (when-not (and (= format-tag (:format entry))\n562\t                 (some #(= (:image entry) (nth % 0)) (:images base)))\n563\t    (refuse-continuation! base entry))\n564\t  (if (:request-sent entry)\n565\t    (-> base\n566\t        (update :wait-set (fnil conj [])\n567\t                (ffi/response-wait-entry entry (:call-id entry)))\n568\t        (assoc :value :yin/blocked\n569\t               :blocked? true\n570\t               :halted? false))\n571\t    (let [call-id (:call-id entry)\n572\t          base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\n573\t          val (if call-id (ffi/call-re